1
00:00:01,080 --> 00:00:06,183
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there are these two views, the view of being and the view of non-being.

2
00:00:07,884 --> 00:00:20,230
[SPEAKER_00]: And the suttas say that anyone who relies on the view of being will be opposed to the view of non-being, and anyone who relies on the view of non-being will be opposed to the view of being.

3
00:00:21,598 --> 00:00:37,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it says, one who does not understand as it really is, the origin, disappearance, gratification, danger, and escape in the case of being and non-being, is affected by lust, hatred, and delusion, and dwells in suffering.

4
00:00:38,906 --> 00:00:45,930
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I wonder what is meant by being and non-being here, and what is the gratification, danger, and escape?

5
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:54,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, in the broadest terms, being is that which is.

6
00:00:55,346 --> 00:01:03,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything that is, anything that can be discerned, perceived, thought about, anything that exists, that is what being is.

7
00:01:04,816 --> 00:01:05,917
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could have like a...

8
00:01:07,062 --> 00:01:13,509
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you could perceive specific things, and there is a form of being, that which you're perceiving.

9
00:01:14,170 --> 00:01:19,055
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you could also start perceiving more general things, such as your experience of perception.

10
00:01:19,535 --> 00:01:20,476
[SPEAKER_01]: You could discern that.

11
00:01:21,377 --> 00:01:22,358
[SPEAKER_01]: And that also exists.

12
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:25,722
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is also a form of being.

13
00:01:26,812 --> 00:01:33,934
[SPEAKER_01]: So, being is not like a static, scientifically measured thing on account of... on the basis of your senses.

14
00:01:34,714 --> 00:01:38,675
[SPEAKER_01]: It's anything that can be mentally discerned as is, including mentality as well.

15
00:01:38,975 --> 00:01:46,637
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if there is a sense of... doesn't matter how vague or how abstract it might feel, it's real as such.

16
00:01:47,777 --> 00:01:48,478
[SPEAKER_01]: That's often said.

17
00:01:49,038 --> 00:01:53,339
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reality of that, say, abstract thought of being that somebody might have in the beginning,

18
00:01:54,784 --> 00:01:58,785
[SPEAKER_01]: That reality of that experience, of thinking abstract thoughts of being, is real as such.

19
00:01:58,985 --> 00:02:00,426
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is already a form of being.

20
00:02:01,166 --> 00:02:04,046
[SPEAKER_01]: That is already a form of existence.

21
00:02:05,607 --> 00:02:15,009
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the Buddha talks about a being, he doesn't talk about specific instances or manifestations of that being.

22
00:02:15,049 --> 00:02:19,270
[SPEAKER_01]: He talks about the whole nature of your experience that has it... that's already a form of being.

23
00:02:19,790 --> 00:02:21,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it exists in a certain way.

24
00:02:22,366 --> 00:02:25,968
[SPEAKER_01]: The five aggregates, the whole, the experience as a whole, and so on.

25
00:02:26,629 --> 00:02:42,077
[SPEAKER_01]: So, fundamentally, on those most general, like, fundamental levels, people will either be adhering to the being, or trying to get rid of it, trying to deny it.

26
00:02:43,478 --> 00:02:43,678
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

27
00:02:48,166 --> 00:02:52,529
[SPEAKER_01]: And, like, say, for example, like, you might be specifically, say, I don't like this.

28
00:02:53,390 --> 00:03:00,035
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might be specifically trying to get rid of that particular being, particular type of existence that you're experiencing.

29
00:03:00,055 --> 00:03:01,456
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to modify it, you want to change it.

30
00:03:02,036 --> 00:03:08,781
[SPEAKER_01]: But, overall, you're very much still enamored and infatuated with the state of existence, with the nature of it.

31
00:03:08,861 --> 00:03:13,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Broadly speaking, you are not... you are very much delighting in that.

32
00:03:15,341 --> 00:03:18,544
[SPEAKER_01]: In your own existence as a whole, like you have no issue with it.

33
00:03:19,485 --> 00:03:22,508
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you could have people that basically start finding issue with that.

34
00:03:23,749 --> 00:03:25,511
[SPEAKER_01]: With their sense of existence as a whole.

35
00:03:26,652 --> 00:03:30,016
[SPEAKER_01]: While on a particular level, they might not be against things.

36
00:03:30,897 --> 00:03:34,140
[SPEAKER_01]: But their general view, it's like they relish the non-being.

37
00:03:35,224 --> 00:03:39,165
[SPEAKER_01]: They delight in the absence of it, when it occurs, and so on.

38
00:03:39,185 --> 00:03:49,788
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, either way, you're bound by that broader sense of being, of your existence, like the five aggregates, the framework of your experience.

39
00:03:50,128 --> 00:04:03,452
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, like, those who fundamentally delight in their own existence, in their own easiness, so to speak, in the broader sense, they will be opposed to any idea of, oh, this will come to an end.

40
00:04:05,397 --> 00:04:06,217
[SPEAKER_01]: This will expire.

41
00:04:06,317 --> 00:04:07,518
[SPEAKER_01]: This could be destroyed.

42
00:04:08,858 --> 00:04:14,540
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, when that being... if that being were to change or cease, they would be opposed to that.

43
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:17,701
[SPEAKER_01]: They would not be... they won't feel that pleasantly, basically.

44
00:04:17,721 --> 00:04:18,902
[SPEAKER_01]: They would feel threatened by it.

45
00:04:19,682 --> 00:04:29,505
[SPEAKER_01]: And, same, those who, for whatever reason, relish the idea, oh, I don't care because everything, you know, including myself, will come to an end and that's... that's... that will be the finish.

46
00:04:29,525 --> 00:04:31,126
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's nothing to worry about anyway.

47
00:04:31,966 --> 00:04:32,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Well,

48
00:04:33,557 --> 00:04:54,285
[SPEAKER_01]: If they were told that, well, actually, that won't be the end, the being will continue, just maybe in another form, they don't relish that idea, because they find solace and freedom, well, freedom, not really freedom, but they find solace and comfort and safety in the idea that, no matter how bad it is, it's going to come to an end and ever after there will be no more problems.

49
00:04:54,346 --> 00:04:57,847
[SPEAKER_01]: When the being ends, that's just complete annihilation and it's the finishing of everything.

50
00:05:00,460 --> 00:05:17,456
[SPEAKER_01]: But either way, what they don't realize, it doesn't matter how hard you think about annihilation and the absence of the being in the most general sense, that experience in of itself, it's a form of being, because it can be discerned and it is in that way.

51
00:05:18,336 --> 00:05:22,240
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not escaping the being, doesn't matter how much you try to destroy it.

52
00:05:24,341 --> 00:05:42,047
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's like the whole point is to discern whether you are relishing finding safety in the existence, or looking forward for it when it ends, because you have conflated that with the idea of how you'll be free ever after, there will be no more problems.

53
00:05:42,347 --> 00:05:43,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you're bound with the being.

54
00:05:45,348 --> 00:05:47,868
[SPEAKER_01]: So, either way, basically, you are mistaken.

55
00:05:50,921 --> 00:06:02,851
[SPEAKER_01]: And that the whole middle way, it's basically recognizing that whether you think existence, whether you think non-existence, that experience is real as such for you there and then, and that thing is.

56
00:06:03,291 --> 00:06:04,252
[SPEAKER_01]: That experience is.

57
00:06:05,252 --> 00:06:19,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you can't just... you won't be able to free yourself and overcome being simply through falling into a view that everything is or everything will not be.

58
00:06:21,409 --> 00:06:28,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're bound by being, not through simply thinking this or that, but through your constant interaction.

59
00:06:29,454 --> 00:06:37,320
[SPEAKER_01]: On the basis of your existence, with other further kind of forms and shapes and manifestations of that existence of the five aggregates.

60
00:06:37,980 --> 00:06:41,923
[SPEAKER_01]: And those interactions are always fueled by greed, lust, aversion.

61
00:06:42,223 --> 00:06:52,430
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for as long as you are acting out of those bases, you are maintaining your bhava, whether you want it or not, whether you want to deny it, whether you believe it will end, whether you believe it will last forever.

62
00:06:53,170 --> 00:06:56,273
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those beliefs are pretty much the results

63
00:06:57,422 --> 00:07:07,049
[SPEAKER_01]: of your engagement with your own sense of being through passion, through aversion, through appropriation, through acquisition, and all of that.

64
00:07:08,170 --> 00:07:16,135
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, being is a problem, because you're not... like, your own being is a problem, because like, oh, I am, but I'm not my own, I'm not in my own control.

65
00:07:16,756 --> 00:07:18,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Whichever way things decide to go,

66
00:07:19,870 --> 00:07:22,372
[SPEAKER_01]: My control can only prevent that only so far.

67
00:07:22,893 --> 00:07:29,158
[SPEAKER_01]: There's always going to be a line where things will be out of my hands, and my, kind of, my existence will be at the mercy of that.

68
00:07:29,939 --> 00:07:46,214
[SPEAKER_01]: So, being is a problem, but then, like, whether you hold a view that, well, being, being is not a problem, or being is a problem, and when it ends I'll be free from the problem, you're still maintaining it, because you're still basically connected with it, through your own actions and through your own ignorance.

69
00:07:47,377 --> 00:07:54,523
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's that whole kind of... the standard description of understanding the gratification, danger and the escape in regard to being.

70
00:07:55,063 --> 00:08:01,968
[SPEAKER_01]: In other suttas, the Buddha said, in regard to sensuality, or in regard to four elements, it was the same description.

71
00:08:02,149 --> 00:08:05,471
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it's the description that basically means discerning the middle ground.

72
00:08:06,232 --> 00:08:07,773
[SPEAKER_01]: So, discerning what the real problem is.

73
00:08:07,793 --> 00:08:09,835
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is not that I find safety in the being.

74
00:08:09,855 --> 00:08:12,437
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is not that I hate the being and I want it to end.

75
00:08:13,303 --> 00:08:16,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Or believe that that would be my freedom and then I find safety in that.

76
00:08:16,587 --> 00:08:24,938
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is that either of those views are the results of my passionate involvement with the being that I am there and then when I think those things.

77
00:08:26,620 --> 00:08:28,963
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, if you address it on that level,

78
00:08:30,287 --> 00:08:37,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Both this kind of eternalist and annihilationist fundamental kind of standpoints, as the Suttas talk about them, will disappear.

79
00:08:37,911 --> 00:08:54,100
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't have to be sort of counter-arguing and reasoning your way out, because you would have removed that wrongful basis that you... wrongful connection, wrongful relationship that you have with your own existence, a relationship of passion, ill-will, distraction, and so on.

80
00:08:54,820 --> 00:08:58,442
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, basically, being will not be anymore owned by you.

81
00:08:59,686 --> 00:09:03,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Assumed through your own actions of the wrong kind.

82
00:09:04,030 --> 00:09:11,656
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, whether that being exists, or when, currently, or where it's going to end, either way, you won't be affected by it, because you escaped it.

83
00:09:13,117 --> 00:09:16,860
[SPEAKER_01]: And you escape it by recognizing the danger of finding safety in it.

84
00:09:19,342 --> 00:09:20,423
[SPEAKER_01]: But see, that's not enough.

85
00:09:20,483 --> 00:09:22,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Just, aha, okay, so being is dangerous.

86
00:09:23,705 --> 00:09:25,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, being free from being is better.

87
00:09:26,548 --> 00:09:26,668
[SPEAKER_01]: But

88
00:09:28,062 --> 00:09:37,304
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless you actually start tailoring your actions accordingly to those reflections, you're just going to fall into a view of blaming the being for your problems.

89
00:09:38,124 --> 00:09:41,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Blaming your existence for the problems, and then thinking when the existence ends, all the problems will end.

90
00:09:43,445 --> 00:09:46,666
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, being is a problem if you're infatuated with it.

91
00:09:47,226 --> 00:09:50,306
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, if you just start to deny it, you're still not changing the problem.

92
00:09:51,007 --> 00:09:52,687
[SPEAKER_01]: You're still under its thumb, so to speak.

93
00:09:53,852 --> 00:09:58,253
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's like, okay, yeah, being infatuated with the being is bad.

94
00:09:58,753 --> 00:10:00,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Denying the being won't free you from that either.

95
00:10:01,534 --> 00:10:02,174
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do I do?

96
00:10:02,214 --> 00:10:03,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what the Buddha recognized.

97
00:10:03,415 --> 00:10:07,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so I recognize, why am I intoxicated with the being?

98
00:10:07,496 --> 00:10:14,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I losing perspective of my actions towards sensuality, towards this, towards that, out of passion, aversion?

99
00:10:15,238 --> 00:10:16,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's because it's like...

100
00:10:17,437 --> 00:10:18,698
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't see carelessness here and now.

101
00:10:19,019 --> 00:10:21,501
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't see the benefit of withdrawing from it.

102
00:10:21,621 --> 00:10:26,706
[SPEAKER_01]: And I don't recognize the connection between those actions and the problem of my existence.

103
00:10:27,587 --> 00:10:30,570
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the whole the Buddha's teaching, explaining that in so many ways.

104
00:10:31,291 --> 00:10:39,920
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the more you start to recognize, then it becomes apparent that, oh, yeah, the only reason you were engaging with it is because he was providing you with a sense of safety.

105
00:10:39,960 --> 00:10:40,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Same with centrality.

106
00:10:42,190 --> 00:10:45,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Providing a form of escape from discomfort, from uneasiness and so on.

107
00:10:46,633 --> 00:10:54,098
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but unless you first renounce that as a form of escape, you won't find the more adequate form of escape.

108
00:10:55,339 --> 00:10:57,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they're kind of the opposite directions.

109
00:10:57,361 --> 00:10:58,862
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can either go left or right.

110
00:10:58,902 --> 00:11:00,763
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't go left and right at the same time.

111
00:11:01,999 --> 00:11:11,004
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, the whole danger, gratification... Well, gratification means that, like, aha, I engage with this because it offers me this superficial safety, that I don't look past and I'm intoxicated with it.

112
00:11:11,765 --> 00:11:12,345
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the danger?

113
00:11:12,365 --> 00:11:13,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the danger is that it's not really safety.

114
00:11:14,086 --> 00:11:16,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Someone says, come in here, this is a safe house, you're safe here.

115
00:11:16,888 --> 00:11:20,830
[SPEAKER_01]: And you might feel safe initially, and then you recognize, oh, no, this was a trap.

116
00:11:20,970 --> 00:11:23,972
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, in this safe house, I am at their mercy.

117
00:11:25,513 --> 00:11:28,355
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you wouldn't lose a second of trying to leave that place.

118
00:11:29,229 --> 00:11:30,570
[SPEAKER_01]: When you recognize the true danger.

119
00:11:30,650 --> 00:11:35,895
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's like knowing the danger, knowing the gratification, which then, if you fully understand, will result in finding the escape.

120
00:11:37,216 --> 00:11:44,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the only reason people don't find escape out of their own existence, from their own existence, is because they still think they are in a place of safety.

121
00:11:45,363 --> 00:11:47,124
[SPEAKER_01]: They still don't see the danger of staying there.

122
00:11:47,665 --> 00:11:53,370
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, how could they possibly find the way out when they don't even look for it?

123
00:11:55,452 --> 00:11:55,772
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now,

124
00:11:57,210 --> 00:12:06,512
[SPEAKER_00]: Sotāpanna understands the origin of suffering as craving for sensual pleasures and also as craving for being and non-being.

125
00:12:07,032 --> 00:12:19,854
[SPEAKER_00]: So, can we say that the craving for sensual pleasure is still on the level of more particular things, but the craving for being and non-being, some more general layer, he has also understood?

126
00:12:20,134 --> 00:12:20,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

127
00:12:20,794 --> 00:12:23,375
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, Sotāpanna understands that craving is the root of suffering.

128
00:12:24,123 --> 00:12:32,284
[SPEAKER_01]: He might not initially be understanding the implications of all... the layers, so to speak, of all craving for sensuality, craving for being, craving for non-being.

129
00:12:32,304 --> 00:12:34,025
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows that craving is a... is an issue.

130
00:12:34,865 --> 00:12:38,425
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the suffering... that's where the suffering comes from.

131
00:12:38,445 --> 00:12:45,527
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't come from things, it doesn't come from the absence of things, it comes from you craving for pleasure, craving away from pain and so on.

132
00:12:46,347 --> 00:12:53,668
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if he then pursues his own understanding of craving is a problem, I... if he pursues the development of the Four Noble Truths, like the Suttas talk about,

133
00:12:54,960 --> 00:13:02,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, he gets to realize that, yeah, basically, your starting position is the domain of sensuality.

134
00:13:02,844 --> 00:13:05,265
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Sotāpanna is still not free from sensual desires.

135
00:13:05,845 --> 00:13:13,529
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he understood craving to be the problem, but he hasn't diminished it sufficiently enough for that domain of sensuality to kind of evaporate, to disappear.

136
00:13:14,209 --> 00:13:16,190
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for somebody who is not free from sensuality,

137
00:13:17,567 --> 00:13:20,708
[SPEAKER_01]: There's really no craving for being or non-being that they can discern.

138
00:13:21,388 --> 00:13:31,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Because any form of affirmation, resistance, eternalism or annihilationism will be well within the kammābhāva, the being of sensuality.

139
00:13:31,351 --> 00:13:35,672
[SPEAKER_01]: That's their domain, that's where the mind operates, that's where their craving is very much still engaged with.

140
00:13:36,432 --> 00:13:45,535
[SPEAKER_01]: But, say, then you make the effort, not giving in to that craving, contemplating the danger and so on, and that craving, by not being fed, will eventually dissipate,

141
00:13:46,561 --> 00:13:50,883
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you would have lost, say, interest in the sense-objects.

142
00:13:52,083 --> 00:13:58,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you recognize that the only reason you were seeking and pursuing interest in sense-objects is because you believe that's where the safety was coming from.

143
00:14:00,927 --> 00:14:08,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, what's left for you will be that bhāvatthāna, envibhāvatthāna, craving for presence, craving for absence, and so on.

144
00:14:09,550 --> 00:14:13,172
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's kind of the level of anāgāmī, the suttas.

145
00:14:15,073 --> 00:14:20,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, in terms of the development towards kind of overcoming the degree of craving.

146
00:14:21,115 --> 00:14:21,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, what's left?

147
00:14:21,875 --> 00:14:33,678
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, yeah, anāgāmī is somebody who still might have some internal leaning of their citta towards the presence of a certain experiences and the absence of a certain experiences.

148
00:14:33,698 --> 00:14:36,719
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I prefer if this were and I prefer if this weren't.

149
00:14:37,479 --> 00:14:48,786
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, at no point will those leanings be able to become what they used to be, like a full-on blown desire towards sense objects as forms of escape from what's been pressuring you here and now.

150
00:14:51,088 --> 00:14:54,270
[SPEAKER_00]: And is this what is meant by the conceit I am?

151
00:14:54,950 --> 00:14:58,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, like that's what conceit I am would be corresponding to.

152
00:14:58,853 --> 00:15:01,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, those subtler cravings that would have been left.

153
00:15:01,875 --> 00:15:02,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

154
00:15:04,912 --> 00:15:11,233
[SPEAKER_00]: And how does an anāgāmī practice in order to abandon even that conceit and attain the complete freedom?

155
00:15:11,413 --> 00:15:12,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Same, same.

156
00:15:12,253 --> 00:15:30,477
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when he starts to recognize those subtler leanings, that factually, like, from the point of view of Putujana, obviously, but even from the point of view of Sotāpanna, from the point of view of, like, even, like, Sakadāgāmī, somebody who is already weak in those things, you could factually say that those leanings are not even an issue.

157
00:15:33,488 --> 00:15:36,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, there's virtually no suffering involved.

158
00:15:36,629 --> 00:15:39,109
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just almost like on the level of agreeable-disagreeable.

159
00:15:40,610 --> 00:15:46,291
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like, often in the suttas, anāgāmīs would think they were arahants.

160
00:15:47,051 --> 00:15:51,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Often the Buddha would say, when they were asked, like, oh, are all these arahants, declare arahantship, are they all arahants?

161
00:15:51,993 --> 00:15:56,454
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, well, I could tell, like, half of them are, but half of them overestimated themselves.

162
00:15:57,302 --> 00:15:59,224
[SPEAKER_01]: But they were factually like anāgāmīs, at least.

163
00:15:59,604 --> 00:16:12,335
[SPEAKER_01]: And even the Buddha himself often would say, practice like this, and it will result in complete arahantship, unless there is some residues left, in which case, state of non-return.

164
00:16:13,375 --> 00:16:18,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, obviously, things get a lot subtler, and there might be some residues that don't even show up, so to speak.

165
00:16:18,960 --> 00:16:20,581
[SPEAKER_01]: But the practice remains the same.

166
00:16:21,362 --> 00:16:23,003
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for an anāgāmī,

167
00:16:23,779 --> 00:16:30,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, he just needs to recognize that there could be residual implications of these mental leanings.

168
00:16:31,464 --> 00:16:32,705
[SPEAKER_01]: And he doesn't need to almost stop this.

169
00:16:32,725 --> 00:16:35,847
[SPEAKER_01]: No, he just needs to regain the same context that brought him there.

170
00:16:36,467 --> 00:16:45,493
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, start including those little preferential kind of almost automatic leaning away, leaning towards, that do not induce any measure of craving.

171
00:16:46,133 --> 00:16:50,836
[SPEAKER_01]: But he includes even that as a possible basis for craving.

172
00:16:50,856 --> 00:16:51,997
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he contemplates the same.

173
00:16:53,077 --> 00:17:00,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Gratification, danger, that then results in not even losing perspective in regard to those subtler things.

174
00:17:00,639 --> 00:17:04,881
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where there will be no conceit left.

175
00:17:05,001 --> 00:17:07,041
[SPEAKER_01]: So, same kind of practice.

176
00:17:07,121 --> 00:17:21,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Just, like, on account of little faith, so to speak, an anāgāmī can then just, out of faith even, start including

177
00:17:22,892 --> 00:17:43,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that do not, as I said, are not problem by any measure, by any kind of comparison to craving, but just in case, he would still practice the same context, maintenance, recollection of the same context, contemplating of the danger, because again, he has nothing to lose by doing that.

178
00:17:43,586 --> 00:17:44,326
[SPEAKER_01]: He can only gain.

179
00:17:45,167 --> 00:17:46,608
[SPEAKER_01]: And if he's already an arahant, well,

180
00:17:47,789 --> 00:17:49,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, he's just confirming what he already is.

181
00:17:50,196 --> 00:17:53,927
[SPEAKER_01]: He's just aligning what his existence already is.

